  "headline": "An oil shock the bond market is fighting with rates, not yet a growth scare",
  "body": [
    "The configuration reads as an inflation shock delivered by oil and met by a hawkish bond market, not a growth scare. Brent at $105 is up 73% year to date, and the 10-year yield at 5.19% sits near the top of its 52-week range. Yet copper is up 18% this year and the copper/gold ratio has climbed 8% over the month: industrial demand has not buckled, which makes this stagflation-lite rather than recession pricing.",
    "Gold is the tell. It is down 4% on the month and sits near the bottom of its yearly range despite a conflict-heavy news flow, because yields are rising faster than fear: higher real returns on cash are beating the haven trade. The dollar index, pressed against its 52-week high, is the other half of that story and explains part of the dollar-priced weakness in gold and copper. Bitcoin buys 9% more gold than a month ago, a sign that liquidity and risk appetite are intact rather than anyone running for cover.",
    "The geopolitics sit in the oil leg. Drop Site News reports an Iranian mine sank a ship in the Strait of Hormuz, MintPress News says the US and Israel are drafting plans for further strikes on Iran with Saudi airspace closed under missile threats, and MintPress also describes a continuing Yemeni Red Sea blockade. The channel is Gulf supply and shipping routes, and the premium is already there: Brent is up 24% over three months with realised volatility a fifth above its own yearly norm. That Brent slipped 1% today on the Hormuz report says the market has largely priced the threat; a confirmed disruption to Gulf flows would still be bullish for Brent, bullish for the dollar, and a headwind for importer currencies such as the rupee and the yen.",
    "The tension is this week. The 30-year yield fell 14 basis points while oil rose 4%, the first hint that bonds are starting to treat the oil shock as a growth hit rather than an inflation problem, and today's selloff in copper and bitcoin with the dollar bid leans the same way. The month-long pattern deserves more weight: the curve has bear-flattened, meaning both yields rose with the 10-year rising faster, which is a market pricing a central bank fighting an oil shock. Two outliers fit that read. The Canadian dollar near its weakest in a year despite $105 oil shows the greenback's rate advantage trumping the commodity bid, while the rupee's 1.3% gain on the week suggests markets do not expect a sustained loss of Gulf supply to Asia."
  ],
